>It's (Stormys) most apparent disadvantages are IMHO
>1. No special ability against undead critters. No life - nothing
>   to drain.

It DOES have special abilities against undead. It drains levels, not
life. Also, it protects YOU against level drain: the main reason why I
choose to wield Stormy when I have him.

>2. It prevents you from NOT killing peaceful creatures; I killed my pet
>   and had to kill a shopkeeper at level 10. (I was fast and heavy
>   armored)

You had to kill a shopkeeper? Why?

>So my questions
>1. Are there other good special weapons for chaotic characters?

I think *Stormy* is good.

>2. What are the effects of wielding non-aligned special weapons?
>   I would like to wish for Mjol(l?)nir, Vorpal Blade or 
>   Grayswandir (which is an underestimated weapon, IMHO). When 
>   you survive the initial blast there seems to be no harm.
>   The only known exception is the Tsurugo of Muramasa, which can
>   be wished for, but which unwinds from the non alligned wielders
>   hand.

You can wish for a quest artifact, but you will only get it if you have
the correct alignment, and it will still blast you because you're not
of the correct class. The Staff of Aesculapius and the Sceptre of Might
behave just like the Tsurugi, by the way.

When you have wished for a non-co-aligned artifact weapon, there is a
one-in-four chance that you will get blasted when you pick it up or
wield it. For Stormy and Excalibur, that chance is 100%, since they are
intelligent weapons.

>3. Another question regarding Stormy: Is drainig life it's only
>   method of increased damage, or does it have an additional 
>   attack bonus?

When a monster loses a level, it also loses 1d8 hit points (one Hit
Dice, that is). But suppose you have a monster with two Hit Dice (that
is, the monster has 2d8 hit points), accumulating to 14 HP maximum. You
hit the monster twice with stormy, for a total damage of 10 HP. Then,
the monster is still dead, since it has now 4 HP but 0 levels. So, the
level drain works in your favour in two ways.

